==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|stān|lang=goh}}, {{term|stēn|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*stāną}}.

===Alternative forms===
* {{term|stehn|lang=de}} {{a|dated in formal prose, but still common informally or poetically}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃteːən/|/ʃteːn/|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|ste|hen|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-stehen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-stehen.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|eːən|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-irregular|steht|stand|gestanden|auxiliary=both|past subjunctive=[[stände]] or [[stünde]]}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[stand]] (to be upright, support oneself on the feet in an erect position) 
# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[be]], to [[stand]] (to be placed or located somewhere)
#* '''1931''', {{w|Arthur Schnitzler}}, ''[[s:de:Flucht in die Finsternis|Flucht in die Finsternis]]'', {{w|S. Fischer Verlag}}, page 36:
#*:Ein frisch gefülltes Glas Champagner '''stand''' vor ihm. Er trank es in einem Zug aus – mit Lust, fast mit Begier.
#*::A freshly filled glass of champaign was in front of him. He emptied it in one draught – with pleasure, almost with greed.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[stay]]; to be [[still]]

====Usage notes====
In Southern Germany, Austria and Switzerland, the auxiliary verb {{term|sein}} is used instead of {{term|haben}}.

